import Native

-- Handing ownership on out of a value that was never moved out of anything.
--
-- Every departure point - a write into another place, a return, a phi input - takes a *value*, and
-- the handover is recorded against whichever slot that value is the whole contents of. A projected
-- load has no such slot: `p.left` and the `v` a `Held(v)` pattern binds are both a read over a path
-- into storage somebody else still owns, so the handover used to find nothing to mark moved, the
-- source was dropped at its last use, and whoever received the bytes dropped them again.
--
-- That is the same statement `->` makes with a projected place, and it is refused for the same
-- reason. The only difference was that `->` said it out loud, so this is where the silent form has
-- to be noticed. Each function below is one shape of it; the expectation beside this file is the
-- whole of what it reports.

data Buffer {bytes: %U8}

instance Drop(Buffer):
    fn drop(->value: Buffer) -> {}:
        freeHeap(value.bytes)

data Pair {left: Buffer, right: Buffer}
data Box = Nil | Held(Buffer)

-- Returning a field. The pair is still owned here and still dropped, so the caller receives storage
-- that is about to be released - and the fix is a move the model does not have yet, which is why
-- this reads as a partial move rather than as anything about the return.
fn returnsField(->p: Pair) -> Buffer = p.left

-- The same through a pattern, and the same silently: `v` refers to the payload, it does not take it.
-- Here the move *is* representable - a record is a discriminant and one payload - so the diagnostic
-- names the spelling that says so.
fn returnsPayload(->b: Box) -> Buffer = match b:
    Held(v) -> v
    Nil -> Buffer {bytes: null()}

-- Into another place rather than out of the frame. The field the pair now holds and the field it was
-- read out of are two owners of one buffer.
fn writesField(->p: Pair, ->q: Pair) -> Pair = Pair {left: p.left, right: q.right}

-- Out of storage this frame only borrows, which is a different mistake: there is no `->` that would
-- make it legal, because nothing here owns the thing to begin with.
fn fromBorrow(b: Box) -> Buffer = match b:
    Held(v) -> v
    Nil -> Buffer {bytes: null()}

-- `->` on something that is not a name. Taking the payload out and *then* asking a question about
-- what came out leaves it moved on the path where the answer was no, with nothing to put it back.
fn testsAfterTaking(->b: Box) -> Buffer = match b:
    Held(->Buffer {bytes: _}) -> Buffer {bytes: null()}
    Nil -> Buffer {bytes: null()}

-- `&` in a pattern. Matching does not establish exclusive access, so there is nothing for the sigil
-- to be true of.
fn mutablePattern(->b: Box) -> {}:
    match b:
        Held(&v) -> {}
        Nil -> {}

-- A boxed payload. `Maybe(Tree)`'s indirection lands on `Just`, so taking the target out would leave
-- the allocation with nothing to hand it back - the reclaim that would have is the one this move
-- just told the compiler not to run.
data Tree = Leaf(Int) | Branch {child: Maybe(Tree), label: Int}

fn takesBoxed(->m: Maybe(Tree)) -> Tree = match m:
    Just(->t) -> t
    Nothing -> Leaf(0)
